	abstract = {University student management faces challenges such as redundant policy documents, inefficient retrieval, and weak knowledge integration. To address these issues, we present a knowledge graph agent that integrates large language models (LLMs) with the Neo4j graph database. Using LangChain as the core framework, the system automatically extracts entities and relations from unstructured policy documents to construct a structured knowledge graph, supporting natural language queries and visual exploration. A multi-agent dialogue simulation mechanism is employed to generate high-quality training data, while an uncertainty knowledge graph (UKG)-based retrieval-augmented method is introduced to enhance robustness in handling ambiguous policies and diverse scenarios. we clarify that the evaluation involved 300 students and counselors from multiple class, producing 680 annotated queries across seven major student management scenarios (e.g., academic, financial aid, daily affairs, mental health, and career planning). The model was evaluated under retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) settings using the RAGAs benchmark, comparing our Neo4j-integrated agent with standard RAG and KG-based frameworks.Experiments show that the proposed approach outperforms conventional retrieval-augmented methods in fidelity, relevance, and contextual consistency, thereby improving the accuracy, coverage, and interpretability of student management applications. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
