	abstract = {Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown strong potential in automating network configuration tasks. However, their outputs often lack the rigor required for safety-critical systems. We propose a configuration synthesis framework that integrates formal planning with LLM-assisted modeling to synthesize device configurations from high-level specifications while ensuring both flexibility and verifiable correctness. The proposed method leverages the Planning Domain Definition Language (PDDL) to formally model routing semantics and state transitions, enabling logical reasoning over network specifications. It introduces a two-stage framework consisting of a common abstraction model for vendor-agnostic protocol reasoning and a vendor configuration model that refines device-level configuration commands through iterative verification and state-based feedback. Experiments on OSPF and BGP networks demonstrate that the proposed approach achieves high compliance with given specifications while ensuring consistency across heterogeneous vendor-specific syntaxes. © 2026 IEEE.},
