	year = {2025},
	journal = {Proceedings of the 18th IEEE/ACM International Conference on Utility and Cloud Computing, UCC 2025},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1145/3773274.3774926},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105027224209?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {As Large Language Models (LLMs) become prevalent in children's applications, ensuring privacy and obtaining informed consent is more critical than ever. Embedding these requirements during the design phase is far more costĝ€'effective than retrofitting compliance afterward. We follow this designĝ€'first principle and introduce a formalĝ€'based approach in which we start with a formalized set of privacy and consent requirements - such as Canada's Personal Information Protection and Electronic Documents Act (PIPEDA) principles, parentalĝ€'consent checks, encryption rules, and dataĝ€'retention policies - then encode them in an executable Alloy model. From there, we derive structured argument patterns directly tied to Alloy Analyzer outputs. This provides endĝ€'toĝ€'end traceability and enables early detection of design flaws. Ultimately, our approach empowers developers to demonstrate compliance and build more trustworthy, privacyĝ€'preserving LLM applications for children. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
