	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105045091584?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Security protocols are fundamental to establishing trust in collaborative systems, particularly within large-scale Internet of Things deployments. However, subtle state machine flaws in their implementations often evade detection by traditional analysis methods. We propose an automated, code-level verification framework powered by Large Language Models (LLMs). The framework utilizes LLMs to extract state transition logic and message semantics from source code into a Protocol Intermediate Language (PIL), while preserving source-level traceability. The extracted PIL is then translated into TLA+ specifications by LLMs via a feedback-driven refinement loop, and subsequently verified using the TLC model checker. We evaluated the framework on widely used TLS and SSH implementations and identified three logic flaws. Notably, we uncovered a state machine logic error in WolfSSL 5.8.2 where the server erroneously accepts a CertificateVerify message before receiving the ClientKeyExchange message. This issue has been confirmed by the vendor. © 2026 IEEE.},
