	abstract = {Synthetic databases are critical for application development, testing, benchmarking, and data engineering workflows. Existing database generators typically require formal specifications, significant technical expertise, or access to production datasets and workloads, making them unsuitable for rapid bootstrapping in greenfield settings. This makes them unsuitable for domain experts seeking rapid bootstrapping in greenfield design settings. To address this, we present ScribbleDB, an end-to-end agentic pipeline that converts natural language descriptions into database schemas and instantiated tables. The pipeline decomposes the task into four stages: fact extraction, schema generation, constraint modeling and code generation. The pipeline consists of four stages: fact extraction, schema generation, constraint modeling, and code generation. By combining LLM-based reasoning with deterministic validation and large-scale materialization, ScribbleDB avoids scalability bottlenecks in schema complexity and data size. Experiments show that ScribbleDB achieves nearly six-fold higher table accuracy and fourfold lower latency than prior work across multiple datasets, while preserving statistical realism and logical fidelity.  © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
