	abstract = {In this paper, we analyse the potential of Large Language Models (LLMs), in particular GPT-4o-mini variants, to automatically infer formal (postcondition) specifications in the design-by-contract (DbC) paradigm. We propose a framework, DbC-GPT, that generates postconditions from textual behavioural descriptions. Although the framework is, in principle, language agnostic, our concrete experiments are in the context of Solidity smart contracts. The inputs to DbC-GPT are (i) a specification of an Ethereum standard in the form of a textual Ethereum Improvement Proposal (EIP) describing the expected behaviour for an Ethereum Request for Comment (ERC) interface, (ii) the interface with the signature of each function to be annotated, and (iii) a reference implementation in the form of a Solidity smart contract. We use a verification tool (solc-verify) in the loop to ensure that the generated specification is both syntactically and semantically valid; semantic conformance is with respect to the reference implementation. To evaluate DbC-GPT, we use some Ethereum standards (ERC20, ERC721, and ERC1155). We then compare the precision of the generated specifications for several demonstration contexts, and datasets for fine tuning, which consider information of these standards in isolation as well as their combination. We compare the evaluation results adopting few-shot learning and fine-tuning. Copyright © 2026. Published by Elsevier B.V.},
