	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035601675?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Gaining a deep understanding of the internal mechanisms of lithium-ion batteries and establishing highprecision battery models are key technologies for assessing the safety status and predicting the lifespan of energy storage systems. Compared to other traditional models, the P2D model can accurately describe the dynamic electrochemical processes of lithium-ion batteries. However, the model consists of complex nonlinear coupled partial differential equations, and how to better solve these equations has become a challenging task. Although PINNs models integrate physical constraints with data-driven approaches to solve P2D models, they face challenges in obtaining required electrochemical data, exhibit poor dynamic adaptability, and underutilize historical information. Therefore, this paper develops an LLM-PISN lithium-ion battery modeling method. Specifically, it first utilizes a large language model to generate pseudo-label values. Battery data is then segmented in the time domain, and spatiotemporal dynamics are predicted using a CNN-LSTM network with historical data. Finally, a P2D model is introduced as a mechanistic constraint to ensure the physical consistency of the final output. Experimental validation demonstrates the effectiveness of the proposed modeling approach.  © 2025 IEEE.},
