	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105045424128?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The rapidly changing cybersecurity landscape requires immediate and precise actions to fight against security incidents. To accelerate the security response development and reduce the risk of human error, Artificial Intelligence techniques, such as Large Language Models (LLMs), are being utilized increasingly often. However, they are being proven to be prone to hallucinations - inaccurate and misleading responses. This drawback makes LLM unreliable as a standalone solution, especially in the context of critical infrastructures and communication systems. The problem can be solved or at least mitigated through a comprehensive and rigorous analysis of the LLM's output. This can be suitably addressed using formal methods, especially formal verification. In this paper, we propose a novel framework to automate cybersecurity incident mitigation by integrating Large Language Models and formal verification (by model checking) techniques. As a result, we provide a proof of concept that illustrates the advantage of the suggested approach and discuss its limitations as well as further developments. © 2026 IEEE.},
