	abstract = {It has always been challenging to handle the numerous requirements while gathering the correct requirements to create software; hence, automating the feature extraction and requirements elicitation processes is essential. Bridging the gap between consumer expectations and the given product, which might be pretty broad, is difficult. Clients commonly communicate their requirements in non-technical language, which is confusing to engineers. The operating technique and tool for automatically extracting and analyzing characteristics from textual requirements specifications provided by users are presented in this study. Using NLP approaches, the program extracts features from user stories and verifies them against a predetermined set of target domain attributes. The extracted characteristics may then create a diagram that provides clients with quick and straightforward input on the intended product. Point of Sales(POS) has been used as a case study to conduct experiments and show the applicability of the proposed method. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Nazmul Siddique, Mohammad Shamsul Arefin, Sheak Rashed Haider Noori, and M Shamim Kaiser; individual chapters, the contributors.},
