	abstract = {Large language model (LLM) serving demands substantial compute and memory resources under dynamic workloads. Model switching has emerged as a promising strategy to balance service quality and system throughput. However, existing model switching approaches typically require restarting the service or recomputing previously generated tokens, causing significant redundant computation. We propose ReKV, a crossprecision model switching mechanism that persistently reuses KV cache states across model transitions. By eliminating prefix recomputation, ReKV enables seamless switching during ongoing generation. ReKV extends FlashInfer CUDA kernels to support multi-segment KV representations without data movement, and employs a layer-wise weight transfer strategy that overlaps weight loading with token generation to reduce switching latency. Experimental results show that ReKV achieves a peak overlap ratio of 13.07% between weight loading and inference, improves system throughput by up to 1.36 ×, and reduces Service Level Objective (SLO) violation rates by 86.59% on average compared with static high-precision configurations. © 2026 IEEE.},
