	abstract = {Remote Direct Memory Access (RDMA) technology is a critical infrastructure for high-performance computing and data centers, yet systematic security testing methodologies for its protocol implementations remain lacking. To bridge this gap, we present Poseidon, an automated property-testing framework for RDMA protocols driven by Large Language Models (LLMs). Poseidon uses carefully designed prompt to extract traceable security properties from Request For Comments (RFC), which drive a Property-Based Testing (PBT) engine integrated with RapidCheck to verify whether RDMA implementations strictly adhere to specifications and identify potential security vulnerabilities. Our evaluation on Soft-RoCE, a mainstream RDMA implementation, demonstrates that Poseidon accurately extracts multidimensional properties from RFC 5040 and successfully detects critical violations and a Kernel Panic. Compared to classical coverage-guided fuzzers, Poseidon achieves higher precision and defect detection capabilities. As the first integration of LLM-driven property extraction with PBT in the RDMA domain, this work establishes a novel, efficacious pathway for verifying consistency between protocol implementations and their formal specifications. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Singapore Pte Ltd. 2027.},
